<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: How Empires Fall</h1><p class="ws-lede">Invasion, division, bankruptcy and revolution: the many ways great powers come to an end.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/how-empires-fall">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Sack of Rome (410)</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Roman Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Fall of Constantinople</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Abbasid Caliphate</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Siege of Baghdad (1258)</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Byzantine Empire</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Roman Empire</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Adrianople, the Visigothic sack of 410 and the loss of North Africa stripped the Western Empire of armies, prestige and revenue.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Byzantine Empire</b>: &#8220;A civilisation that saw its own decline clearly: the loss of Anatolia and the crusader sack of 1204 did damage it never repaired.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 9, <b>Ottoman Empire</b>: &#8220;The conqueror of Constantinople endured until defeat in the First World War and the abolition of the sultanate in 1922.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Invasion, division, bankruptcy and revolution: the many ways great powers come to an end.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/how-empires-fall. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: How Empires Fall</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Roman Empire <span>from 27 BCE</span></li><li>Byzantine Empire <span>from 330 CE</span></li><li>Sack of Rome (410) <span>410 CE</span></li><li>Abbasid Caliphate <span>from 750 CE</span></li><li>Siege of Baghdad (1258) <span>1258</span></li><li>Fall of Constantinople <span>1453</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
